From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 04:50:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] zink: free a cached buffer when it expires, not only when
 another is cached

pb_cache frees an expired buffer in two places only: pb_cache_add_buffer()
frees the expired buffers of every bucket, and pb_cache_reclaim_buffer()
those of its own bucket.  A client that frees buffers and then stops
allocating therefore leaves every one of them cached for as long as it stays
idle, although Zink asks for them to expire after 500 ms.  On a KosmicKrisp
host an idle Android guest was measured holding 25 to 30 MiB of Zink
dedicated heaps this way, and about 75 MiB for the seconds after its apps
closed; the same run with the cache limit set to zero held none of it.

pb_cache gains the two entry points an idle cache lacks.
pb_cache_release_expired() frees what has expired in every bucket, and
pb_cache_next_expiry_ms() reports the time until the earliest expiry (0 when
a buffer already has, -1 when the cache holds none).  A bucket keeps its
buffers in the order they were added, which is the order they expire in, so
the first buffer of each bucket is enough.  Both take the cache's mutex like
the paths that exist.  pb_cache_add_buffer() also asserts that the cache
holds no more than its limit after an addition.

pb_cache_reaper is the thread that uses them: it sleeps until the earliest
expiry and frees what has expired, and with an empty cache it sleeps with no
deadline until pb_cache_reaper_wake() reports an addition.  The wake is a
load of one flag unless the thread sleeps without a deadline, because a
buffer added while it waits for a deadline expires after the buffer that set
it.  The thread sets the flag under its lock, looks at the cache once more
and then waits, while an addition is in the cache before it reads the flag,
so one of the two always sees the other; the cache's mutex orders them.  A
non-empty cache costs one wake-up per expiry, up to two a second at Zink's
500 ms, and an empty one nothing.  Another driver that calls neither entry
point is unaffected.

Zink starts one reaper per screen in zink_bo_init() and signals it from
bo_destroy_or_cache() once a buffer is in the cache.  zink_bo_deinit()
stops it after the slabs are released, since releasing a slab's buffer adds
it to the cache, and before the cache is destroyed.  A screen whose reaper
could not start behaves as it did.

src/gallium/auxiliary/pipebuffer/pb_cache_reaper_test.c checks the expiry
arithmetic across buckets, that the reaper frees a buffer within a second of
its last addition with nothing added or reclaimed, that a reaper asleep with
an empty cache wakes for every new buffer, that a reclaimed buffer is never
freed behind its owner, that stopping a reaper does not wait for a distant
expiry, and that four threads adding and reclaiming against a reaper and a
small cache destroy every buffer exactly once and never hold more than the
limit.  It is built into the gallium suite with -Dbuild-tests=true and
-Db_ndebug=false, and runs under -Db_sanitize=thread.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
